turning to afghanistan where the taliban  says it's speaking directly to the u.s  about building relations more than a  month after american and foreign forces  withdrew but in an interview with al  jazeera the taliban's foreign affairs  spokesman denounced u.s drone operations  in afghan airspace  their violation of our territorial  integrity of afghanistan  it is a blatant and a clear violation  it is against  the commitments that the united states  of america made  in the doha agreement  the doha agreement clearly stipulates  that  the united states and  other countries as well  its allies will not interfere  in the internal affairs of afghanistan  they are not only violating their own  commitments but they are also violating  international law  for more on this let's go live now to  hashimoto who is in kabul hashem so on  the one hand the taliban is denouncing  u.s activity  in afghan airspace but but is also  speaking directly with washington what  which should we make of all of this  well basically kim this  shows the dilemma that all the parties  are facing in afghanistan the taliban  took over they won the war but suddenly  they found themselves grappling with how  to run the country they need resources  they need cash they need assets that are  frozen in the u.s they need the  international recognition to be able to  move forward in the absence of that they  will have a huge problem financial and  political and one day they won't be able  to feed their own people and this  explains why they are desperate for  international recognition they had high  hopes that the americans would step in  but the americans are waiting to see  whether the taliban of today are not the  taliban of 1996 when they first took  over  the country and this explains why on one  hand the taliban are upset with the  americans because they have intercepted  what they say are drones which have been  flying over the airspace of afghanistan  and they say this should stop but at the  end of the day we've been told by many  taliban officials that there are direct  talks with the americans and they're  hoping to convince the americans to deal  with the taliban for them that will be a  cornerstone that would pave the way for  more stability in afghanistan  hashem in this interview it was also  discussed the rights of of women and  girls  uh and their civic engagement or role in  civic society what was said on that  front  well i have to say there is  there seems to be a divide within the  taliban about how to tackle issues  particularly  giving women a bigger say in the  government women's role and gender  segregation  and the decision to put restrictions on  girls education and to deny  female secondary students from going to  school has backfired in a way or another  even those who've been asking to end the  isolation of taliban like qatar and  pakistan have been very critical lately  of the taliban saying that it's about  time for the taliban to understand that  there are realities on the ground they  have to readjust with they have to  reinvent themselves to be able to move  forward and this explains why  when when i spoke with abdul al-qahab  the spokesman of the taliban's minister  foreign affairs  he is saying exactly that he we should  give the taliban some time to figure out  how to move forward let's listen to what  he had to say  we have committed and we have guaranteed  all the rights  that have been afforded to women in  islam  that is their right to education their  right to health care their right to work  and  they  the the process  that the world wants us  uh  the steps they once want us to take we  are taking them we have seen progress in  them we understand all the complexities  of of the  of the current world  we understand it very well and we are  working  but again i must emphasize that  as the saying goes rome was not built in  a day  we understand the world we understand  the different complex political  realities as abdul qahar balahi said  but then we have to tell  our viewers that in 1996 when the  taliban took over they were a different  group run by one single person the emir  alma minero the commander of the  faithful mullah roma along with tribal  leaders and clerics and then they had a  different version a different worldview  about how afghanistan should stand they  thought that a puritanical conservative  society would be the best way to protect  afghanistan today you have different  views within the taliban you have those  who say  we've been doing business with different  key players in the world and we have to  understand that if we don't reinvent  ourselves we will never be able to move  forward but you have those  hard-line taliban leaders those from the  1996 era who still say that if we make  concessions when it comes to identity  sharia allah islamic laws giving women  more freedom that could  undermine our reputation but that also  could create some sense of uh uh  discourse among the uh the conservatives  who remain i have to say the backbone of  the taliban movement will they be able  to set aside those differences and move  forward well i don't know at this  particular moment but from talking to  many many taliban leaders they do  understand that this time  the options left for them are very few  and there's absolutely no way they can  move forward and get international  recognition if they don't make  concessions  thank you for that update in kabul
 

